WEBVTT

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.800
All that is within me, Franklin Roosevelt once wrote, cries out to go back to my home

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:27.620
on the Hudson River. Hyde Park was the center of the world.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.000
this program to bring

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:09.940
a special news bulletin from cbs world news association has just announced that president

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:17.610
roosevelt is dead the president died of a cerebral hemorrhage all we know so far is that the

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:30.000
president died at warm springs in georgia on april 13th 1945 the funeral train headed

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:36.970
The last car lay the body of the President of the United States.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:41.190
Franklin Delano Roosevelt had led Americans through the Great Depression and the greatest

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.670
war in history.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.170
Now along railroad tracks from Georgia to New York, they gathered to say goodbye.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:57.490
A whole generation of Americans had grown up knowing no other president.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.000
He was a presence in their living rooms.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.300
friends. He'd been at the helm through the two worst crises of this century, and to

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.190
have him suddenly gone was an overwhelming shock.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:24.960
The boss man come to the field and he threw up his hand. I was flying a tractor. He said, "Mr. Rodevelt died today."

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.870
I said, "What?" He said, "Mr. Rodevelt died today. I just sat there."

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.260
I just sat there astounded.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:35.620
I felt like I had lost one of the closest brothers

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.650
I ever had.

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:44.730
- It was the single greatest feeling of loss,

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:50.610
disorientation, uncertainty, and the sense

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.310
that the whole world was now without the one man

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.510
that it needed.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.870
- This was a man of great ebullience.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.650
He was a man of constant cheer.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.840
He was a man of laughter.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.850
He had the feeling of life.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:13.840
There was vitality.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.360
This was a country in despair,

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.200
and he brought us all together.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:44.190
He was the man with the big, easy smile, the infectious sense of humor.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.470
President, how soon are you coming back?

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:48.930
Just as soon as Congress will let me.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:55.820
He loved conversation, company, and good times.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:57.520
Last year, I didn't care.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:11.570
this was how americans saw franklin roosevelt this was the man they trusted so much they elected him

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:18.300
president four times people just idolized him the most astounding thing was the pictures

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:23.250
of roosevelt you saw everywhere bus stations libraries barbershops

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:29.760
homes there were pictures of roosevelt the entire countries decided he was the savior

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:38.660
We face the future with confidence and with courage. We are Americans.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:48.080
Never before Roosevelt had Americans felt that government would take care of them, protect their homes and their farms,

00:04:48.780 --> 00:05:00.000
guarantee their savings accounts, promise them security in sickness and old age. But the president who

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:15.600
championed the common man was not like most Americans.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.920
My dear Mama, I am in a great hurry.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.060
I found two birds' nests.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.120
I took one egg.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:30.000
Your loving Franklin.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.810
Eleanor Roosevelt spent his childhood among people so unlike ordinary Americans, they

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.670
modeled themselves after the lords and ladies of England.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:54.690
The privilege that FDR grew up with was one that was essentially very comfortable for

00:05:54.690 --> 00:06:00.000
everybody, and the families that lived on those estates.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:07.130
dates, were generally friends with one another, related very often to each other, and were

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:11.550
the only people that visited one another.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:17.770
I think it's fair to say that even the professional men in the towns who were the doctors and

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:25.350
the lawyers and so on were not generally invited to the river houses to dinner.

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:30.000
Franklin Roosevelt was born in Hyde Park, New York on January 30, 1886.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.990
A big forested estate his parents called Springwood.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:40.550
Springwood was a beautiful, isolated place.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:43.260
It was its own world.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:48.860
And it was entirely built around this privileged little boy.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:07:00.000
And I think he spent most of his life trying to replicate the way his boyhood was arranged.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.980
"At the outset, he was plump, pink and nice," his mother said.

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:10.800
"I used to love to bathe and dress him.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:13.340
"He looked very sweet, his little blonde curls bobbing

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:15.520
"as he ran as fast as he could,

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:19.790
"whenever he thought I had designs on combing them."

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:22.390
Nearly every detail of Franklin's childhood

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.890
was recorded with single-minded devotion

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:29.010
by his mother, Sarah Delano Roosevelt.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:30.000
She kept his baby clothes,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:37.330
clothes, every childish drawing, each golden curl. Franklin was eight and a half years old

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:38.950
before he was allowed to bathe himself.

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:54.230
If it's the job of a mother to make her child feel that he or she can do anything, then Sarah

00:07:54.230 --> 00:08:00.000
Delano Roosevelt was surely one of the great mothers in American history. Franklin's father was more

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:08.350
than 25 years older than sarah he was 53 when franklin was born franklin called him popsy

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:18.290
everyone else called him mr james mr james bred trotters and rode to the hounds he smoked cheroots

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:24.370
he would ride out with his son to survey their estate workers tip their hats to mr

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:30.000
james and then to master franklin the boy accepted these displays of

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:43.220
deference as routine. FDR grew up in a very tight little island. He learned how to please adults

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:52.780
from probably before he remembered. His activities were related to showing off for them, relating to

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:59.580
them, not to other children. And he didn't go off to play games with other children.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:05.980
I don't think he ever swung a baseball bat until he finally went to school.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:14.480
He was tutored at home or abroad because every year they went abroad for several months.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:30.000
FDR, with all his attention, was undoubtedly a lonely boy.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:36.060
wandered his family estate, secure, he later said, in the peacefulness and regularity of things.

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:50.910
Then, when he was nine, his well-ordered world fractured. His 63-year-old father suffered a

00:09:50.910 --> 00:10:00.000
heart attack. Any irritation might aggravate him, provoke another heart attack, and kill him. His

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.040
sickness must have reinforced the tendency that was already in him as a small child to be a nice

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:13.140
boy to never make any trouble never make anybody sad now he had to worry if i go in there and make

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:19.290
trouble i may weaken his already weakened heart so it must have put an enormous pressure on this kid

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:26.260
with an infirm father and a domineering mother franklin learned to conceal his true feelings

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:30.000
throughout his life he would remain a charming

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.960
but distant figure, even to those who were closest to him.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:50.400
When he was 14 years old, Franklin left the rarefied world of his Hyde Park estate.

00:10:52.580 --> 00:11:00.000
His path seemed clear. Boarding school, Harvard, and an uneventful life of luxury and

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:11.610
ease among his own kind. Dear Mama, I am getting on very well with the fellows. I have not had any

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:18.730
black marks or lateness yet, and I'm much better in my studies. His letters are always cheerful.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:24.000
Everything's wonderful. He's having a grand time with the other fellows, and yet he wasn't.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:30.000
He was, I think, quite unhappy. At Groton, a private school for sons of

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.820
of the rich, Franklin, with all his charm and self-assurance,

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:36.290
expected to excel.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:38.940
He did please his teachers and took to heart

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:42.640
his headmaster's urgings toward public service.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:46.410
But he did not fit in with the boys.

00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:50.240
- Groton was his first exposure to other children

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.460
on a regular basis, after all.

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:55.350
He boarded, all the children boarded.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:59.760
So he was with other boys 24 hours a day.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:05.430
And it must have been a rude shock to come out of that nest,

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:11.200
that very protected nest, where he was the only bird or chick in the nest.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:23.950
Sports meant everything at Grotten, but Franklin was too slight for success.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:29.920
His mother worried Franklin might be injured and wrote that he'd not have the misfortune of hurting anyone.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:37.700
He was enthusiastic about baseball, but only carried the bats and fetched the water for the ballplayers.

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:43.740
He wasn't an athlete. He had never played with other boys' games much.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:47.920
And that was very bad, indeed, because it made him an outsider.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:53.740
As if he wasn't, you know, as if he didn't belong. And really, in a strange way, he didn't belong.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:13:00.000
Plunged into an unforgiving world of adolescent boys, Franklin never fit in.

