00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:10.450
But at nights, Roosevelt, Eleanor reported, would be tormented by nightmares, and he was sleepwalking, and then he broke out in hives, all of which suggested that something wasn't right.

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:26.190
They climbed the Alps, motored through the French countryside. Few would have sensed that they were ill at ease.

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:34.410
He loved to have a good time.

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:36.290
All his life, he loved to do that.

00:25:36.290 --> 00:25:39.450
She wanted someone she could confide in.

00:25:39.450 --> 00:25:41.570
She'd been alone, really, all her life,

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:43.930
and she wanted an intimate partner.

00:25:43.930 --> 00:25:45.790
She did not get one, and Franklin,

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:49.260
he didn't like sharing intimacies with anyone,

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:50.130
even his wife.

00:26:01.660 --> 00:26:10.970
back in america sarah was waiting i am so glad sarah had written them that although you've had

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:19.950
such a perfect time you are now anxious to see home and mother again that winter as a christmas

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:25.970
gift sarah gave them a drawing with a note scrawled on the bottom to franklin and eleanor

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:29.860
from mama number and street not yet quite decided

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:34.460
when franklin and eleanor arrive home from their honeymoon sarah tells them i've got a present for

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:40.310
you it's great a new home not only that but it's furnished by her it's structured by her

00:26:40.310 --> 00:26:49.240
it's decorated by her it's her sarah built them a brownstone and then moved into its twin next door

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:55.690
the house is five stories and on each floor there are sliding doors where she can walk

00:26:55.690 --> 00:27:00.000
from her side of the house into their side and uh eleanor

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:03.670
Roosevelt writes, "There was never any privacy, day or night.

00:27:03.670 --> 00:27:07.920
Sarah Delano Roosevelt was just part of the scene."

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:11.590
In a way, a great-grandmother made her dependent.

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:15.450
She wanted both her son and her daughter-in-law

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:17.810
to be dependent upon her.

00:27:17.810 --> 00:27:22.280
His mother controlled FDR's purse strings

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:27.210
until the day she died in 1941,

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:29.730
when he was into his third term as president.

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:37.420
But the President of the United States didn't control his own income, his mother did.

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:12.180
The first ten years of her married life were spent having children, six children in about

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:13.870
ten years.

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:20.420
She loved the children, but she didn't really know how to run that infant stage.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.930
I think she was totally inept when it came to dealing with children.

00:28:34.930 --> 00:28:41.510
She relied on her mother-in-law and on the various governesses and was so unsure of herself,

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:47.170
not only because she was an unsure person at the time, but she had never experienced

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:50.590
mother love.

00:28:50.590 --> 00:28:55.490
And because she felt insecure about not knowing how to mother her own children, she once again

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:57.590
turned to Sarah.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:06.200
Sarah knew. Sarah had confidence. Sarah had an opinion about everything. So it wasn't

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:11.040
only that they brought in nurses and grannies to help the children. Sarah was the overseer

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:22.760
of the house entirely and of the kids. His mother's ever looming presence was never

00:29:22.760 --> 00:29:30.000
challenged. FDR never wanted to move out of his mother's home. And so they lived

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:35.590
lived in Sarah Delanor Roosevelt's homes. At Hyde Park, it's her home. So she sits at

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:41.370
the head of the table. Franklin sits at the other end of the table. And Eleanor sits somewhere

00:29:41.370 --> 00:29:46.020
in the middle with the children.

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:54.930
I see her as an upper-class grand dame who knew her place. She was just doing what came

00:29:54.930 --> 00:30:00.000
naturally. She, in a way, knew who she was.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:05.230
And my grandmother, in the early years of her marriage, didn't know who she was.

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:09.590
That took a long time for her to find out.

00:30:09.590 --> 00:30:15.580
In the early 1900s, the Roosevelts appeared to be an utterly conventional upper-class couple,

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:21.600
with Franklin amiably dabbling in the law.

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:27.400
But at 25, he was bored and restless, looking for an outlet for his enormous energies.

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:34.200
To a fellow law clerk, he confided a remarkable secret ambition.

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:41.660
He said he intended to enter political life as soon as he could, with a view to becoming

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:42.580
president.

00:30:42.580 --> 00:30:52.710
He said that modestly enough, but very definitely, and he laid out a definite plan.

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:58.390
Franklin told Clark he would follow the path blazed by his hero, Theodore Roosevelt.

00:30:58.390 --> 00:30:59.380
State legislature?

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:09.110
Assistant Secretary of the Navy, Governor of New York, President of the United States.

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:14.290
Cousin Theodore had already proved that a gentleman might, as Franklin's mother said,

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:18.740
go into politics but not be a politician.

00:31:18.740 --> 00:31:22.220
Theodore Roosevelt was almost an obsession with Franklin.

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:27.080
When he was told he had to wear glasses, he got past nay and put them on his nose because

00:31:27.080 --> 00:31:30.000
Theodore Roosevelt wore past nay.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:35.120
things like bully and delighted when he was talking to the press early in his

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:41.590
political career. He was fascinated by his energy, his enthusiasm, above all I

00:31:41.590 --> 00:31:45.430
think in his feeling that government could do enormous amounts of good.

00:31:45.430 --> 00:31:51.540
Theodore Roosevelt was the great model for Franklin Roosevelt. In 1910 at the

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:55.820
age of 28, Franklin jumped at the chance to follow in his cousin Theodore's

00:31:55.820 --> 00:31:59.760
footsteps. He was invited to run for the State Senate.

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:02.080
mostly because his last name was Roosevelt.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:06.770
He ran as a Democrat, although T.R. was a Republican.

00:32:08.580 --> 00:32:11.520
His father had been a Democrat, but I think the real reason

00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:14.720
was that Theodore Roosevelt had several sons,

00:32:14.980 --> 00:32:19.700
all of whom everyone presumed were going to have political careers in the Republican Party,

00:32:19.960 --> 00:32:24.130
and there was simply not enough room for another Republican Roosevelt.

00:32:24.130 --> 00:32:29.670
He was offered...

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:39.200
the impossible task of running for office in Dutchess County. No Democrat had ever

00:32:39.200 --> 00:33:00.000
been elected in 32 years. A very good speaker in those early days, there would be hard

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:09.270
horrible long pauses, and I would wonder whether he was ever going on again.

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:20.500
He made a very vigorous campaign, and it just happened that that year was a democratic sweep.

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:30.000
And he got in. Otherwise, I don't think he would have started then at all.

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:35.800
Franklin celebrated by handing out $14 worth of good cigars.

00:33:39.060 --> 00:33:42.080
In Albany, in the rough-and-tumble world of state politics,

00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:45.200
he began his career in the style of his cousin Theodore.

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:47.960
Within days of being sworn in,

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:50.860
he led a rebellion against the leadership of his own party.

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:53.980
He lost, and the bosses never forgave him.

00:33:55.940 --> 00:33:57.900
Party regulars couldn't stand him.

00:33:57.900 --> 00:34:00.000
They thought he was rich, smart.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:09.180
Unwilling to compromise or cooperate a snob. This fellow is still young one of them said

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:14.270
Wouldn't it be safer to drown him before he grows up to survive?

00:34:14.830 --> 00:34:20.540
Franklin would need help and he turned to a shrewd strange-looking reporter Louie how?

00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:27.200
I remember the smell of Louie how more than anything else a gnome

00:34:27.940 --> 00:34:30.000
gone sure

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:36.450
short-wispy hair, I mean, enough to scare a child, and I was.

00:34:36.450 --> 00:34:41.540
He's dirty. He never showers or bathes enough.

00:34:41.540 --> 00:34:47.100
He smokes these dreadful, smelly, sweet corporal cigarettes

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:52.430
in the ashes, you know, sort of coat his vest and tie.

00:34:52.430 --> 00:34:54.960
Franklin's mother especially disliked him.

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:57.540
That "dirty little man," she called him.

00:34:57.540 --> 00:35:00.000
Eleanor, too, disapproved.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:06.000
He represents the worst, the smelliest, you know, stuff of politics.

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:08.000
He drinks, he smokes, he curses.

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:11.020
He's a pain. Out of there.

00:35:11.020 --> 00:35:15.480
But still, Louis Howe was a seasoned politician.

00:35:15.480 --> 00:35:18.480
As you might say, he knew where all the bodies were buried.

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:20.530
NFDR needed to know.

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:25.900
Together, Howe and Franklin formed one of the oddest alliances in American political history.

00:35:25.900 --> 00:35:30.000
It would last until Howe's death in 1936.

00:35:31.180 --> 00:35:36.160
I was so impressed with Franklin Roosevelt, Howe liked to say of their first meeting,

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:40.990
I thought then, nothing but an accident could keep him from becoming President of the United

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:48.950
States.

00:35:48.950 --> 00:35:54.290
In 1913, after only two years in Albany, the Democratic state senator with the famous last

00:35:54.290 --> 00:36:00.000
name was summoned to Washington. Impressed by his growing reputation as a reformed

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:05.950
Democrat and by Franklin's pedigree, President Woodrow Wilson offered him the job of Assistant

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:12.180
Secretary of the Navy, the same job that Theodore Roosevelt had used to catapult himself to

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:30.000
the presidency. He was just 31 years old. Franklin loved the Navy. He pressed for the largest possible flee

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:35.790
He learned to deal with congress, businessmen, labor.

00:36:35.790 --> 00:36:47.250
And he built a reputation as enthusiastic, efficient, hard working.

00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:53.530
But just as he began to walk the corridors of real power, first he put his job and then

00:36:53.530 --> 00:36:56.340
his marriage in jeopardy.

00:36:56.340 --> 00:36:59.410
Washington for Franklin is a great liberation.

00:37:00.030 --> 00:37:02.710
You know, he never had a teenage rebellion.

00:37:02.710 --> 00:37:07.110
He never had a moment where he defied his mother or his wife.

00:37:07.110 --> 00:37:10.210
He had really been a dutiful son,

00:37:10.210 --> 00:37:12.800
and he had really been a dutiful husband.

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:16.470
Washington blew all that out of the water,

00:37:16.470 --> 00:37:19.310
if I may use a naval term for the assistant secretary

00:37:19.310 --> 00:37:23.080
of the Navy.

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:26.370
He was a young man on the make.

00:37:26.370 --> 00:37:30.000
He worked for Secretary of the Navy Josephus Daniels, and he

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.340
wanted his job, ridiculed him behind his back,

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:36.290
undermined his decisions.

00:37:36.290 --> 00:37:40.230
- FDR worked as a subordinate under Josephus Daniels

00:37:40.230 --> 00:37:42.560
for almost eight years.

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:44.920
And he was a terrible subordinate.

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:47.840
I think he simply couldn't stand the notion

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:49.380
that someone was giving him orders

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:53.210
about something he was quite sure he knew much more about.

00:37:53.210 --> 00:37:56.910
Roosevelt undercut his boss time and again.

00:37:56.910 --> 00:38:00.000
He went over his head to the president from time to time.

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:03.290
And Daniels put up with all of it.

00:38:03.290 --> 00:38:07.140
Daniels said he enjoyed Franklin's spontaneity and gaiety,

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:10.290
imagining great things for him.

00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:12.900
When they looked at their picture taken together,

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:16.900
Daniels told him, "I'll tell you why you're smiling.

00:38:16.900 --> 00:38:19.080
We're both looking down on the White House,

00:38:19.080 --> 00:38:21.350
and you're saying to yourself, someday,

00:38:21.350 --> 00:38:24.070
I will be living in that house."

00:38:24.070 --> 00:38:26.750
Franklin just kept smiling.

00:38:26.750 --> 00:38:30.000
Daniels thought Roosevelt a wonderfully charming young man,

00:38:30.600 --> 00:38:33.880
i think must have been the most patient man in american history because

00:38:35.240 --> 00:38:42.280
any other man would have fired roosevelt for insubordination early on at the same time

00:38:42.280 --> 00:38:50.630
franklin's marriage was heading for trouble it was not a happy household fdr enjoyed himself he

00:38:50.630 --> 00:38:59.630
enjoyed having a good time and unfortunately he couldn't get my grandmother to go along she actually

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:07.370
disapproved. She had moral reservations is the only way I can put it about really

00:39:07.370 --> 00:39:15.920
enjoying herself. Eleanor was caught in what she described as the slavery of the

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:25.120
Washington social system, dutifully advancing her husband's career. Overwhelmed

00:39:25.120 --> 00:39:30.000
with social obligations, she spent her days leaving her calling cards at the stately homes of

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:39.350
the rich and powerful. I was perfectly certain, Eleanor later wrote, that I had nothing to offer

00:39:39.350 --> 00:39:45.470
and that my duty as the wife of a public official was to do exactly as the majority of women were

00:39:45.470 --> 00:39:51.880
doing. And suddenly the most important thing is to be part of the social world of Washington, D.C.,

00:39:51.880 --> 00:40:00.000
which is essentially a round of cocktail parties, trivial conversation, the very thing that Eleanor hates. Franklin finds out that

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:03.380
he's incredibly well-suited for the small-talk,

00:40:03.380 --> 00:40:06.390
gossipy side of Washington life.

00:40:06.390 --> 00:40:09.570
He's a great conversationalist, he loves telling stories,

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:12.450
he loves small talk, and he loves that kind

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:14.630
of superficial connection between people,

00:40:14.630 --> 00:40:19.200
and his vitality and his magnetism are beginning to show.

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:22.410
- Franklin was especially attractive to women.

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:24.590
One Washington hostess described him

00:40:24.590 --> 00:40:28.550
as the most desirable man she had ever met.

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:43.710
Every summer, the Roosevelt seemed to find relief

00:40:43.710 --> 00:40:45.210
from the strains of Washington

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:48.560
on an island off the coast of Maine, Campobello.

00:41:06.490 --> 00:41:09.530
We've spent so little time alone with our parents,

00:41:09.530 --> 00:41:11.840
their eldest son James later wrote,

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:20.590
that those times are treasured as though gifts from the gods.

00:41:20.590 --> 00:41:24.980
Father loved life on the island more than any of us,

00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:26.980
got to spend the least time there.

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:36.190
Mother always liked it because she had her own home,

00:41:36.190 --> 00:41:43.170
which she ran.

00:41:43.170 --> 00:41:47.400
Father taught us to sail.

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:50.610
This was the one activity he loved above all others

00:41:50.610 --> 00:41:54.420
and wanted us to love.

00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:56.500
But as summer after summer went by,

00:41:56.500 --> 00:41:59.510
Franklin spent less and less time at Campobello.

00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:02.620
Eleanor grew anxious and suspicious.

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:08.080
In the summer of 1917, Franklin wrote from Washington to calm her.

00:42:10.070 --> 00:42:15.330
Dearest Babs, you were a goosey girl to think, or even pretend to think,

00:42:15.490 --> 00:42:18.630
that I don't want you here all summer because you know I do.

00:42:20.850 --> 00:42:24.990
But honestly, you ought to have six weeks straight at Campo, just as I ought to.

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:27.460
Only you can and I can't.

00:42:50.510 --> 00:42:52.610
When America entered World War I,

00:42:52.610 --> 00:42:55.050
the Navy sent Franklin on an inspection tour

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:56.180
of the Western Front.

00:43:05.020 --> 00:43:08.270
He reviewed the troops, toured the battlefields,

00:43:08.270 --> 00:43:09.670
and got as close to the fighting

00:43:09.670 --> 00:43:16.640
as the military would allow him.

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.680
As he was about to sail back to America,

00:43:18.680 --> 00:43:21.440
he was struck down by a strain of influenza

00:43:21.440 --> 00:43:26.180
and brought home severely ill.

00:43:26.180 --> 00:43:29.570
Franklin was 36 years old.

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:32.780
He had handsome children, a dutiful wife,

00:43:32.780 --> 00:43:39.600
a famous name, and a rising career.

00:43:39.600 --> 00:43:42.010
As Eleanor unpacked his suitcase,

00:43:42.010 --> 00:43:43.810
she accidentally made a discovery

00:43:43.810 --> 00:43:46.710
that would change their lives forever,

00:43:46.710 --> 00:43:50.760
a packet of love letters to her husband.

00:43:50.760 --> 00:43:55.170
Lucy Page Mercer was Eleanor's own social secretary.

00:43:55.170 --> 00:43:56.830
She was a refined young woman

00:43:56.830 --> 00:43:58.990
from an old Southern Catholic family.

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:03.200
Lucy was tall and statuesque.

00:44:03.200 --> 00:44:06.720
She had a face, people say, that belonged in drawing rooms.

00:44:06.720 --> 00:44:10.720
She had a charm that was rivaled only by Franklin's charm.

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:14.430
One thinks of Franklin in those days, and indeed throughout his life,

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:16.350
as this incorrigible flirt.

00:44:16.350 --> 00:44:20.570
Flirting was a part of his vitality, his magnetism, his charm.

00:44:20.570 --> 00:44:23.930
He loved to conquer women in conversation.

00:44:23.930 --> 00:44:26.250
So that's probably how it started with Lucy.

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:28.930
But then I do think it became something more.

00:44:34.350 --> 00:44:39.540
While Eleanor was away at Campobello, Franklin spent time with Lucy alone and

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:44.580
even appeared with her at dinner parties. He had fallen in love.

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:53.590
Eleanor Roosevelt really had a very romantic

00:44:53.590 --> 00:45:00.000
idea that she could have a perfect marriage, that they would love and trust and

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:04.600
respect each other, and be partners in love,

00:45:04.600 --> 00:45:06.700
the way her parents never were.

00:45:06.700 --> 00:45:09.100
Which is, I think, why her discovery

00:45:09.100 --> 00:45:19.160
of the Lucy Mercer affair was so devastating to her.

00:45:19.160 --> 00:45:22.000
- The bottom dropped out of my own particular world,

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:24.360
Eleanor confided to a friend.

00:45:24.360 --> 00:45:27.940
And I faced myself, my surroundings, my world,

00:45:27.940 --> 00:45:29.510
honestly for the first time.

00:45:31.660 --> 00:45:38.660
Eleanor's immediate response was not only to confront Franklin, but from what we seem to understand, to offer him a divorce.

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:42.150
If this is what you want, go.

00:45:42.150 --> 00:45:49.910
Mr. Roosevelt knew that his mother would withdraw for all financial help. She threatened him with that.

00:45:49.910 --> 00:45:59.030
He would lose his family life, and it really meant giving up his political ambitions.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:05.220
And that was something he had to think over more than once.

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:10.300
Franklin would have to choose between his love for Lucy Mercer and his political career,

00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:13.590
family, and Eleanor.

00:46:13.590 --> 00:46:20.750
And he finally decided to stay married and to try to make the best of a marriage.

00:46:20.750 --> 00:46:27.190
And Mrs. Roosevelt's condition was that he never see Lucy Mercer again.

00:46:27.190 --> 00:46:29.830
After everyone had their say, James later wrote,

00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:36.990
father and mother sat down and agreed to go on for the sake of appearances. Eleanor and

00:46:36.990 --> 00:47:00.000
Franklin would live together but never again share the intimacies of married life. Devastated,

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:07.990
went time and again to Washington's Rock Creek Cemetery.

00:47:07.990 --> 00:47:10.310
For hours, she sat gazing at a monument

00:47:10.310 --> 00:47:17.170
to a woman who had killed herself.

00:47:17.170 --> 00:47:20.820
All her childhood fears had been confirmed.

00:47:20.820 --> 00:47:23.800
Those she loved most, first her father,

00:47:23.800 --> 00:47:27.960
now her husband, would always desert her.

00:47:27.960 --> 00:47:28.870
Nothing lasted.

00:47:36.460 --> 00:47:39.320
It was a mock turning point in her life.

00:47:39.320 --> 00:47:41.590
She had no persona.

00:47:41.590 --> 00:47:44.220
She felt destroyed.

00:47:44.220 --> 00:47:48.120
She'd have to make a life for herself.

00:47:48.120 --> 00:47:50.390
And that's what she did.

00:48:03.200 --> 00:48:08.080
The surprise of the 1920 Democratic Convention was the nomination for vice president.

00:48:08.080 --> 00:48:15.650
The young man I am going to suggest, the speaker said, has a name to be conjured with in American politics.

00:48:17.300 --> 00:48:23.260
Franklin Delano Roosevelt was 38, one of the youngest vice presidential candidates in American history.

00:48:23.920 --> 00:48:26.980
His cousin Theodore had been 42 at this point in his career.

00:48:27.460 --> 00:48:29.180
Franklin was now ahead of schedule.

00:48:42.280 --> 00:48:47.950
As the campaign got underway, he threw open his mother's Hyde Park home for a party rally.

00:48:47.950 --> 00:48:56.020
Sarah was proud, but appalled when 5,000 loyal Democrats trampled her lawn and invaded her stately home.

00:49:08.990 --> 00:49:13.340
Vice presidential candidates usually ran modest campaigns.

00:49:13.340 --> 00:49:16.020
Franklin barnstormed more than 8,000 miles

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:27.130
through 20 states in 18 days.

00:49:27.130 --> 00:49:30.000
During the three months in the year 19

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.680
He said I got to know the country is only a candidate for office or a traveling salesman can get to know it

00:49:40.150 --> 00:49:43.470
Franklin pressed Eleanor to accompany him on the campaign

00:49:44.850 --> 00:49:59.380
Reluctantly she went along hated every minute of it the smoke-filled rooms the late-night card games the hard-drinking politicians and reporters and

00:50:02.270 --> 00:50:05.010
Ever since her discovery of her husband's affair,

00:50:05.010 --> 00:50:08.510
she had been cautiously embracing a life of her own.

00:50:08.510 --> 00:50:11.930
Before Lucy came into their lives,

00:50:11.930 --> 00:50:15.850
my own sense is that Eleanor was not happy simply as a wife and a mother,

00:50:15.850 --> 00:50:17.930
but she had no outlet for her energy.

00:50:17.930 --> 00:50:21.150
She had torrential energy and there was no outlet for it

00:50:21.150 --> 00:50:23.570
because in that day and age, it wasn't legitimate

00:50:23.570 --> 00:50:27.130
for a woman to have a career outside the home.

00:50:27.130 --> 00:50:30.000
The affair with Lucy Mercer enabled her

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:39.550
to see herself in perhaps a different light. I personally believe that FDR's affair with

00:50:39.550 --> 00:50:47.250
Lucy Mercer enabled my grandmother to open a door and walk out.

00:50:47.250 --> 00:50:52.830
She meets with all of these political women. They were suffragists, they were progressives,

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:56.070
they are dedicated to making things better for most people.

00:50:56.070 --> 00:51:00.000
They were women who knew things, who could educate her, who could teach her parliamentary

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:07.730
law who could tell her about labor movements labor unions and so on it also

00:51:07.730 --> 00:51:14.720
was slightly rebellious of her she was breaking bounds Eleanor was moving into

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:20.250
a vanguard of women who were political activists in 1920 women were voting in

00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:25.560
their first national election and Franklin never missing a political beat

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:27.620
was ardently courting their vote

00:51:30.570 --> 00:51:34.290
He loved every minute of the 1920 campaign.

00:51:34.290 --> 00:51:36.090
But when an aide asked him if he thought

00:51:36.090 --> 00:51:40.470
he would be elected, he replied, "Nary an illusion."

00:51:40.470 --> 00:51:43.890
For the Democrats, the election was a disaster.

00:51:43.890 --> 00:51:47.370
For the Republicans, the victory, one observer said,

00:51:47.370 --> 00:51:53.430
was more than a landslide, it was an earthquake.

00:51:53.430 --> 00:51:58.160
But for Franklin, the campaign was a triumph.

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:00.000
Americans all across the country

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:05.870
now knew his name.

00:52:05.870 --> 00:52:07.570
He had met and won the goodwill

00:52:07.570 --> 00:52:11.020
of thousands of party leaders.

00:52:11.020 --> 00:52:12.760
He stood ready to aim higher

00:52:12.760 --> 00:52:18.710
than the vice presidency next time.

00:52:18.710 --> 00:52:28.030
At 38, he was young, strong, energetic, and impatient.

00:52:28.090 --> 00:52:30.000
In the summer of 1921,

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:37.770
he visited a boy scout camp serving city children he enjoyed himself immensely posing for pictures

00:52:37.770 --> 00:52:45.160
for the newspapers and joking with the boys this is the last photograph of franklin roosevelt

00:52:45.160 --> 00:52:52.810
standing on his own two feet when he said goodbye he took with him the goodwill of the campers

00:52:52.810 --> 00:52:59.800
and a mysterious undetected virus already multiplying and circulating throughout his body

00:53:19.640 --> 00:53:21.720
August 14th, 1921.

00:53:24.280 --> 00:53:28.060
We have had a very few anxious days, Eleanor wrote from Campobello.

00:53:31.530 --> 00:53:35.260
"Wednesday evening Franklin was taken ill.

00:53:35.260 --> 00:53:39.000
"By Friday evening he lost the ability to walk

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:42.130
"or move his legs.

00:53:42.130 --> 00:53:45.190
"The doctor feels sure he will get well,

00:53:45.190 --> 00:53:52.170
"but it may take some time.

00:53:52.170 --> 00:53:54.970
"I tried to persuade myself that the trouble

00:53:54.970 --> 00:53:58.020
"with my leg was muscular, Franklin remembered,

00:53:58.020 --> 00:53:59.890
"that it would disappear as I used it.

00:54:00.830 --> 00:54:06.740
But presently, it refused to work, and then the other.

00:54:06.740 --> 00:54:12.340
It started with a cold, a feeling of malaise,

00:54:12.340 --> 00:54:16.970
an ache in the back, and a lack of appetite,

00:54:16.970 --> 00:54:19.710
and said he thought he wouldn't have dinner,

00:54:19.710 --> 00:54:24.640
and he went up to his room, and he never walked again.

00:54:32.400 --> 00:54:52.890
ultimately at the high point he was helpless. For a man as energetic who had

00:54:52.890 --> 00:55:00.000
led such a charmed life to suddenly be paralyzed must have been all

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:12.070
almost unbearable. He asked Louis Howe why God had deserted him at one point. He tried

00:55:12.070 --> 00:55:17.290
to put on a brave front with the children, but he was terrified. They didn't know what

00:55:17.290 --> 00:55:23.730
it was. They didn't know what they could do about it. Certainly, it was the blackest moment

00:55:23.730 --> 00:55:27.440
of his life and seemed to be the end of his life.

00:55:30.730 --> 00:55:35.130
His fever soared. Eleanor remembered he was out of his head.

00:55:35.130 --> 00:55:40.620
Eleanor responds immediately with help, with support,

00:55:40.620 --> 00:55:43.840
with courage in facing the severity of what he's going through.

00:55:43.840 --> 00:55:47.640
She stays up 24 hours. She's by his side.

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:53.750
She doesn't run away from it. Desperate, Eleanor called in an orthopedic specialist

00:55:53.750 --> 00:55:55.250
from Harvard Medical School.

00:55:55.250 --> 00:55:59.730
The diagnosis, he told them, was perfectly clear.

00:56:01.180 --> 00:56:03.930
Infantile paralysis, polio.

00:56:07.130 --> 00:56:10.440
He couldn't believe this had happened to him.

00:56:11.730 --> 00:56:17.930
But even in those circumstances, he kept to the Roosevelt code, which was that you did not complain.

00:56:18.500 --> 00:56:21.580
And that you tried to convince everyone that everything was going to be fine.

00:56:22.460 --> 00:56:25.780
He was very careful to be cheerful in front of his mother.

00:56:26.880 --> 00:56:30.000
And she was very careful to be cheerful in front of him.

00:56:30.480 --> 00:56:33.180
And only after she left him did she cry.

00:56:37.880 --> 00:56:41.630
Private railroad car brought Franklin home to New York City.

00:56:43.100 --> 00:56:45.960
With every curve and jounce, he winced in pain.

00:56:47.360 --> 00:56:48.900
He was 39 years old.

00:56:49.900 --> 00:56:52.960
No one knew what sort of life might now be possible for him.

00:56:53.790 --> 00:56:55.180
But one thing seems certain.

00:56:55.960 --> 00:56:57.680
His political career was finished.

00:57:00.030 --> 00:57:01.390
He was an aristocrat.

00:57:01.880 --> 00:57:02.920
He was well-born.

00:57:03.100 --> 00:57:05.300
He was good-looking.

00:57:05.520 --> 00:57:06.980
He had always had everything.

00:57:07.980 --> 00:57:14.790
All of a sudden, there he was, crippled in a day when it was a very difficult thing to be crippled.

00:57:16.000 --> 00:57:19.830
In the 1920s, polio was a terrifying thing.

00:57:20.650 --> 00:57:26.230
Something like 25% of the people who caught polio died of it within the first two weeks.

00:57:26.720 --> 00:57:30.000
If you survived and you had paralysis, they didn't know what to do.

00:57:30.860 --> 00:57:37.810
And nice families kept their disabled members at home in the back bedroom with the blinds

00:57:37.810 --> 00:57:52.510
drawn with a certain shame attached to it somehow.

00:57:52.510 --> 00:58:00.000
His mother decided that the best thing to do was for him to come home to Hyde Park and

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:07.550
the life really that his father had lived as an invalid she would take care of him and he could

00:58:07.550 --> 00:58:17.870
pursue his hobbies and small interests but he would have to give up politics it's one of those

00:58:17.870 --> 00:58:24.050
moments that is absolutely a defining moment for eleanor because she knows that if sarah has her

00:58:24.050 --> 00:58:30.000
way franklin's soul will be destroyed so she has to do something she's never done before

00:58:30.090 --> 00:58:33.990
She has to confront Sarah directly to tell her you're wrong and I'm not going to let

00:58:33.990 --> 00:58:34.990
this happen.

00:58:34.990 --> 00:58:38.490
He's going to be able to get out of this house, he's going to walk again, he's going to get

00:58:38.490 --> 00:58:41.190
into politics, and I don't care what you say.

00:58:41.190 --> 00:58:45.330
She may not have said it that boldly, but she definitely was willing to make a major

00:58:45.330 --> 00:58:49.290
confrontation with her mother-in-law that all of her earlier life she had been unable

00:58:49.290 --> 00:58:52.930
to make.

00:58:52.930 --> 00:58:58.430
She became the voice for his inner needs, his inner feelings, and in some ways that's

00:58:58.430 --> 00:59:00.000
what she becomes all of their life.

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:09.180
eyes she had a discipline and a willpower that was staggering she said to me once the only time

00:59:09.180 --> 00:59:16.560
in my life that i cried in his presence when he had polio was when he called us into the room

00:59:16.560 --> 00:59:24.900
and he showed us look he said what i can do he was phobic about being caught in a fire

00:59:24.900 --> 00:59:30.000
helpless in a fire he got himself down from the bed

00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:39.210
And he showed them with great pride how he slithered on the floor using his elbows to get to the door.

00:59:39.720 --> 00:59:43.260
And with that, Mrs. Roosevelt broke down in tears and fled.

00:59:43.440 --> 00:59:47.620
And she said that was the only time she didn't control herself in front of him.

00:59:49.130 --> 00:59:50.910
Franklin's life was now in limbo.

00:59:50.910 --> 00:59:58.790
He would devote the next seven years to one single goal, to get back on his feet.

01:00:03.800 --> 01:00:08.930
The doctors told him that his only hope was exercise.

01:00:08.930 --> 01:00:12.230
With heavy steel braces grappled to his legs,

01:00:12.230 --> 01:00:19.880
he began the awkward struggle to learn to walk.

01:00:19.880 --> 01:00:22.830
"I must get down the driveway today."

01:00:22.830 --> 01:00:24.810
"All the way down the driveway,"

01:00:24.810 --> 01:00:26.560
his daughter Anna heard him say.

01:00:30.510 --> 01:00:42.970
polio exercises were very painful very tedious humiliating they took up endless time and i think

01:00:42.970 --> 01:00:52.100
it's a measure of his ambition and his grit that he kept at them as long and as hard as he did after

01:00:52.100 --> 01:00:58.260
a year of unrelenting struggle the doctors told franklin it was all but certain that he would never walk again

01:01:00.250 --> 01:01:03.470
but he would not accept their verdict.

01:01:03.470 --> 01:01:05.650
The rules that applied to other people

01:01:05.650 --> 01:01:07.790
did not apply to Franklin Roosevelt,

01:01:07.790 --> 01:01:11.870
and he refused to believe that he was not going to get better.

01:01:11.870 --> 01:01:15.570
He tried everything-- sun lamp treatments,

01:01:15.570 --> 01:01:18.150
special electric belts that were supposed

01:01:18.150 --> 01:01:22.710
to make him somehow stronger, pulley arrangements

01:01:22.710 --> 01:01:25.750
to do his exercises automatically.

01:01:25.750 --> 01:01:30.000
Deep massage, light massage, range of motion exercises,

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:34.180
Sometimes they hung him on a harness from the ceiling.

01:01:34.790 --> 01:01:36.850
And even in the last weeks of his life,

01:01:37.450 --> 01:01:41.390
he was trying a new method to see if he couldn't get back on his feet.

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:46.530
It's either madness or enormously admirable.

01:01:47.940 --> 01:01:55.820
He never spoke to anyone about the feelings he had with his paralysis.

01:01:56.710 --> 01:01:59.540
His mother said that he had never spoken to her about it.

01:02:00.890 --> 01:02:05.030
Eleanor said he simply didn't accept his paralysis

01:02:05.030 --> 01:02:08.270
and he didn't talk about it, and he wouldn't admit it.

01:02:08.270 --> 01:02:11.010
I suppose psychologists might call it denial.

01:02:11.010 --> 01:02:13.770
It certainly served him well

01:02:13.770 --> 01:02:16.030
and allowed him to become president of the United States

01:02:16.030 --> 01:02:19.260
instead of a stamp-collecting invalid.

01:02:19.260 --> 01:02:21.800
Of course it's denial.

01:02:21.800 --> 01:02:25.840
It worked, and denial is a very useful thing in its place.

01:02:25.840 --> 01:02:30.000
I mean, it's a way of coming to terms

01:02:30.730 --> 01:02:39.120
with a difficult fact.

01:02:39.120 --> 01:02:48.240
In 1924, Franklin bought a rundown houseboat

01:02:48.240 --> 01:02:51.590
he named the LaRocco and headed south.

01:02:51.590 --> 01:03:00.000
He was running away, drifting lazily

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:06.570
off the coast of Florida, swimming, fishing, cavorting with his friends. He filled his days

01:03:06.570 --> 01:03:08.190
with aimless good times.

01:03:15.380 --> 01:03:30.000
He was there partly to exercise and get the sun, which he thought was going to help.

01:03:30.540 --> 01:03:41.560
But he was also there because life at home with five children and his mother and his wife fighting over him was unbearable.

01:03:47.610 --> 01:03:51.020
These were very grim years for him, for the family.

01:03:54.220 --> 01:03:55.420
He was struggling to get better.

01:03:56.020 --> 01:03:58.760
In spite of his optimism, he really wasn't getting much better.

01:04:03.520 --> 01:04:11.950
think that the guy was dealing with depression. There's a great deal of anger, a great deal

01:04:11.950 --> 01:04:18.790
of grieving, a great deal of frustration that comes with paralysis. Extensive, severe, serious

01:04:18.790 --> 01:04:26.550
paralysis. And this is very hard stuff to deal with.

01:04:26.550 --> 01:04:30.000
And he couldn't express that despair and that sadness around Ellen.

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:33.100
certainly not around his mother, nor around the kids.

01:04:33.100 --> 01:04:35.660
So I think he went to the LaRocco originally

01:04:35.660 --> 01:04:39.560
because it allowed him a haven to be able to be sad,

01:04:39.560 --> 01:04:43.350
to mourn the loss of the body that had once been his.

01:04:43.350 --> 01:04:45.970
And I think it took longer than he thought.

01:04:45.970 --> 01:04:57.870
There were days on the LaRocco, one friend remembered,

01:04:57.870 --> 01:05:00.000
when it was noon before he could pull up

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:04.580
himself out of depression and greet his guests wearing his light-hearted facade.

01:05:04.580 --> 01:05:14.300
"The polio was a storm," one of his physiotherapists said.

01:05:14.300 --> 01:05:17.600
"You were what remained when it had passed."

01:05:35.600 --> 01:05:37.240
His son James later wrote,

01:05:37.970 --> 01:05:39.450
These were the lonely years.

01:05:40.130 --> 01:05:41.710
We had no tangible father,

01:05:41.870 --> 01:05:44.210
no father whom we could touch and talk to,

01:05:44.910 --> 01:05:46.470
only a cheery letter writer.

01:05:48.760 --> 01:05:52.280
With Franklin gone and the children away at school or grown up,

01:05:52.900 --> 01:05:56.860
politics was becoming an increasingly important part of Elnor's life.

01:05:57.460 --> 01:06:00.000
Louis Howe convinced her that it was up to her to

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:05.580
keep Franklin's name alive. If Eleanor had not stepped forward, Franklin's political

01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:08.250
career might well have been over.

01:06:08.250 --> 01:06:15.090
Louis Howe decided that I'd better go in the women's division of the state political

01:06:15.090 --> 01:06:23.720
setup in New York and I must be able to speak. And so Louis Howe used to go with me to meetings

01:06:23.720 --> 01:06:30.000
and sit in the back and make fun of me afterwards. Louis Howe is absolutely central to

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:32.400
Eleanor Roosevelt's political education.

01:06:33.050 --> 01:06:35.010
He monitors her every word.

01:06:35.130 --> 01:06:38.230
He attends all of the speeches that she gives.

01:06:38.770 --> 01:06:41.510
And he tells her, you said this right, you said that wrong.

01:06:41.570 --> 01:06:42.430
You giggled here.

01:06:42.550 --> 01:06:43.690
Why did you giggle here?

01:06:43.930 --> 01:06:46.630
Your voice went up 10 decibels here.

01:06:46.750 --> 01:06:47.750
Why did it do that?

01:06:47.790 --> 01:06:48.990
He's really her coach.

01:06:48.990 --> 01:06:54.230
And she very quickly, in two or three years,

01:06:54.760 --> 01:06:57.300
moved to the top in the women's division

01:06:57.300 --> 01:06:59.500
in the New York State Democratic Party.

01:07:00.280 --> 01:07:04.000
She could get up and talk on pretty much any subject,

01:07:04.000 --> 01:07:06.950
and with some ease, and she talked informally.

01:07:06.950 --> 01:07:18.820
She ran a very good meeting.

01:07:18.820 --> 01:07:22.480
Eleanor discovered that she had organizing skills,

01:07:22.480 --> 01:07:25.970
a talent for dealing with people,

01:07:25.970 --> 01:07:27.730
and soon found herself in the thick

01:07:27.730 --> 01:07:29.600
of Democratic Party politics.

01:07:33.840 --> 01:07:37.470
Publicly, she spoke in Franklin's name.

01:07:37.470 --> 01:07:50.420
Privately, she was developing her own ideas.

01:07:50.420 --> 01:07:55.910
- Go to her for advice.

01:07:55.910 --> 01:07:57.410
People go to her to raise money.

01:07:57.410 --> 01:07:59.440
People go to her and ask her to speak.

01:08:01.240 --> 01:08:06.240
She says in her memoirs, she's done it all for FDR, but the fact is, she loves every minute of it.

01:08:06.240 --> 01:08:16.970
I think she was profoundly impressed and believed in her heart that helping other people, enabling other people,

01:08:16.970 --> 01:08:24.770
particularly when you were in a position of privilege, was the way she wished to conduct her life.

01:08:24.770 --> 01:08:30.000
Eleanor was making all the great reform causes of the day her own.

01:08:30.760 --> 01:08:34.520
child labor, public housing, workers' compensation,

01:08:34.520 --> 01:08:36.250
unemployment insurance.

01:08:36.250 --> 01:08:40.020
She was becoming a voice for those who had none.

01:08:40.020 --> 01:08:44.230
She had abdicated her role as wife.

01:08:44.230 --> 01:08:48.810
She had. He invited her very many times to come down.

01:08:48.810 --> 01:08:52.840
But he was with the friends whom she didn't especially care for,

01:08:52.840 --> 01:08:57.040
the congenial friends, the friends from the Lucy Mercer days,

01:08:57.040 --> 01:09:00.000
the people who liked to live on a houseboat,

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:06.150
and swim and drink, and he was with Missy LeHand.

01:09:07.660 --> 01:09:14.060
Missy was Marguerite LeHand, Franklin's secretary.

01:09:14.620 --> 01:09:18.370
Unmarried, Catholic, high school educated, many years younger.

01:09:20.870 --> 01:09:24.590
Missy had started working for Franklin when she was 20 years old in 1920,

01:09:25.050 --> 01:09:29.120
and I think fell in love with him and never stopped loving him all the rest of her life.

01:09:32.940 --> 01:09:34.700
It would be Missy who would sit by his side

01:09:34.700 --> 01:09:35.940
as they went fishing.

01:09:35.940 --> 01:09:38.420
She learned every activity that he liked

01:09:38.420 --> 01:09:41.420
and became an expert at it.

01:09:41.420 --> 01:09:43.020
So she was the perfect companion

01:09:43.020 --> 01:09:48.570
for these lazy, aimless days on the LaRocco.

01:09:48.570 --> 01:10:00.000
Eleanor is nowhere in sight during this period of time.

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:03.000
Eleanor had now made a life of her own with her own friends.

01:10:03.000 --> 01:10:09.630
"Alas and lackaday," wrote her aunt, "I just hate to have Eleanor let herself look as she does.

01:10:09.630 --> 01:10:16.860
Since politics have become her choicest interest, all her charm has disappeared."

01:10:16.860 --> 01:10:19.860
Franklin supported Eleanor's newfound independence.

01:10:19.860 --> 01:10:24.290
During the 1920s, they had once again redefined their marriage.

01:10:24.290 --> 01:10:29.320
They were bound together by politics, respect, and real affection.

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:37.460
they led separate lives. For the first time, Eleanor had a home of her own, two miles from

01:10:37.460 --> 01:10:43.570
Hyde Park, a simple cottage built for her by Franklin. She called it Valkill, after

01:10:43.570 --> 01:10:52.280
the brook running past its door. She can invite who she wants there. Her mother-in-law

01:10:52.280 --> 01:11:00.000
has to knock before she enters. There are no sliding doors. Sarah is shocked that Eleanor

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:04.770
Roosevelt would prefer to live in what she calls that hovel rather than the

01:11:04.770 --> 01:11:11.620
proper house, the big house with the proper number of servants. Eleanor Roosevelt's

01:11:11.620 --> 01:11:30.000
very happy at Val Kill. Val Kill, Eleanor felt free. She defied convention, befriending so-called

01:11:30.090 --> 01:11:35.860
called "New Women" who lived with one another.

01:11:35.860 --> 01:11:39.140
Eleanor found in these friends the kind of emotional closeness

01:11:39.140 --> 01:11:48.600
that Franklin could not provide.

01:11:48.600 --> 01:11:51.850
All through the 1920s, Eleanor flourished.

01:11:51.850 --> 01:11:53.730
She became financially independent,

01:11:53.730 --> 01:11:56.340
writing articles for newspapers and magazines.

01:12:00.800 --> 01:12:09.860
She taught at a private school in New York, which she co-owned and continued to campaign

01:12:09.860 --> 01:12:14.780
for progressive political causes.

01:12:14.780 --> 01:12:17.360
She traveled widely.

01:12:17.360 --> 01:12:22.500
She was at ease with herself and for the first time in her life began to have fun.

01:12:55.400 --> 01:13:00.000
Salkill was Eleanor's, but throughout his life, Franklin was a frequent and welcome

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:09.160
guest.

01:13:09.160 --> 01:13:20.420
Eleanor's friends became his friends, supporting his hopes to one day return to political life.

01:13:20.420 --> 01:13:24.040
"I don't want him forgotten," Eleanor said.

01:13:24.040 --> 01:13:26.120
"I want him to have a voice."

01:13:30.510 --> 01:13:36.960
In 1924, the former candidate for vice president was invited to the Democratic National Convention.

01:13:36.960 --> 01:13:43.110
He delivered a rousing speech, but he played no further part in the presidential campaign.

01:13:43.110 --> 01:13:46.040
He was still far too weak.

01:13:46.040 --> 01:13:49.750
That fall, there was talk of Franklin running for governor of New York,

01:13:49.750 --> 01:13:51.860
but he quickly rejected the idea.

01:13:51.860 --> 01:13:57.310
He would not seek public office, he said, until he no longer needed crutches.

01:13:57.310 --> 01:13:59.540
Determined to find a cure,

01:14:00.030 --> 01:14:08.370
he once again headed south.

01:14:08.370 --> 01:14:11.350
He had heard of pools of steaming mineral water

01:14:11.350 --> 01:14:12.910
in Warm Springs, Georgia,

01:14:12.910 --> 01:14:14.910
whose marvelous healing powers

01:14:14.910 --> 01:14:22.050
were the stuff of local legend.

01:14:22.050 --> 01:14:23.910
Gushing out of the side of a mountain,

01:14:23.910 --> 01:14:27.490
the waters were 90 degrees and astonishingly buoyant.

01:14:31.470 --> 01:14:38.050
Some called them miracle waters.

01:14:38.050 --> 01:14:41.870
Although the waters would never give Franklin back his legs,

01:14:41.870 --> 01:14:50.360
they would give new meaning to his life and new purpose.

01:14:50.360 --> 01:14:51.880
I feel, he wrote his mother,

01:14:51.880 --> 01:14:54.760
that a great cure for infantile paralysis

01:14:54.760 --> 01:14:58.200
and kindred diseases could well be established here.

01:15:03.520 --> 01:15:10.510
Well, there wasn't much here. There was only one hotel downtown, you know, and a grocery store, and they called it Bullockville.

01:15:10.840 --> 01:15:14.940
It wasn't considered a town, what I would call a town.

01:15:15.740 --> 01:15:21.180
I thought it took a lot of store to make a town back in those days, but I just called it a greasy spot.

01:15:21.180 --> 01:15:29.420
And I think they named it Warm Springs, Georgia, after Mr. Roosevelt, about the time he began to come here.

01:15:31.020 --> 01:15:35.440
on the outskirts of town there stood a once lovely vacation resort a favored

01:15:35.440 --> 01:15:41.670
retreat for wealthy southerners by the time franklin arrived its glory had faded

01:15:41.670 --> 01:15:47.470
to a cluster of cottages in need of repair and a rundown hotel franklin

01:15:47.470 --> 01:15:51.380
dreamed of restoring its original charm and turning it into a modern

01:15:51.380 --> 01:15:56.440
rehabilitation center for those with infantile paralysis but first he would

01:15:56.440 --> 01:16:00.000
need hundreds of thousands of dollars roosevelt borrowed

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:05.640
lot of money from his mother and put in a lot of his own. His wife was absolutely opposed

01:16:05.640 --> 01:16:09.680
and thought it was a terrible idea. She thought it was going to cost too much money. She thought

01:16:09.680 --> 01:16:16.090
that it would give him something else to attend to and take him away from politics, that you

01:16:16.090 --> 01:16:23.010
couldn't do everything and Warm Springs was going to completely dominate his life. She worried

01:16:23.010 --> 01:16:28.780
about it. One of the few times we know in which he really got angry at her was when she gently

01:16:28.780 --> 01:16:30.000
suggested that perhaps

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:33.600
This was not a good idea, and he was furious.

01:16:33.600 --> 01:16:39.060
He really exploded and said, "This is something I really want.

01:16:39.060 --> 01:16:41.520
You either support me or you don't."

01:16:41.520 --> 01:16:47.600
And then when that was sort of an emotional ultimatum, she did support it.

01:16:47.600 --> 01:16:54.140
Investing $195,000, two-thirds of his personal fortune, Franklin created and designed the

01:16:54.140 --> 01:17:00.000
first modern treatment center for infantile paralysis in the country.

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:04.390
with polio from across America were making the pilgrimage to the Georgia

01:17:04.390 --> 01:17:19.450
backwoods. As Franklin struggled to rehabilitate his own withered limbs, he

01:17:19.450 --> 01:17:22.500
devoted himself for the first time to helping others.

01:17:42.440 --> 01:17:44.880
- You would howl with glee, he told a friend,

01:17:44.880 --> 01:17:47.200
if you could see the clinic in operation

01:17:47.200 --> 01:17:50.240
and the patients doing various exercises in the water

01:17:50.240 --> 01:17:53.830
under my leadership.

01:17:53.830 --> 01:17:56.500
- Warm Springs was a wonderful place.

01:17:56.500 --> 01:17:59.000
Roosevelt was a patient just like all the others.

01:18:00.120 --> 01:18:03.680
Dr. Roosevelt, as the others called him,

01:18:03.680 --> 01:18:06.150
was really remarkably creative.

01:18:06.150 --> 01:18:11.120
He brought in blacksmiths and they designed braces and crutches,

01:18:11.120 --> 01:18:15.010
a crutch design that still used the Warm Springs crutch.

01:18:15.010 --> 01:18:19.650
Roosevelt invented a muscle testing technique,

01:18:19.650 --> 01:18:24.100
a way of grading how strong a muscle is that is still in use.

01:18:24.100 --> 01:18:28.070
And it was a remarkably inventive time

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:37.150
Always, he believed in recovery, his prescription, swimming, sunlight, and belief on the patient's

01:18:37.150 --> 01:18:39.010
part that the muscles are coming back.

01:18:42.900 --> 01:18:48.750
He had charisma.

01:18:49.090 --> 01:18:54.000
He was just glowing with it, and he'd throw that smile, and he'd laugh.

01:19:13.760 --> 01:19:20.930
After everybody had treatment, they would all go out in what was called a play pool and they would play vigorous games of ball.

01:19:25.010 --> 01:19:29.030
He played with them and he was just as tough as any of the children. They loved him.

01:19:38.990 --> 01:19:43.310
Whether or not people got better at Warren Springs, they felt that they were better.

01:19:43.990 --> 01:19:49.400
And they felt that with him present, anything was possible.

01:19:55.340 --> 01:19:57.380
Warren Springs was Franklin's creation.

01:20:00.160 --> 01:20:02.920
For the rest of his life, in times of stress,

01:20:02.920 --> 01:20:06.990
he would retreat to the piney woods and the warm waters.

01:20:06.990 --> 01:20:17.630
It became his second home.

01:20:17.630 --> 01:20:23.910
Franklin loved to drive and he drove fast.

01:20:23.910 --> 01:20:27.170
He designed his car himself with ingenious levers

01:20:27.170 --> 01:20:30.000
and pulleys so he could drive without his legs.

01:20:32.270 --> 01:20:34.630
For the first time since he was paralyzed,

01:20:34.630 --> 01:20:37.140
he felt free.

01:20:37.140 --> 01:20:40.080
Over the years, his drives through the Georgia countryside

01:20:40.080 --> 01:20:46.260
would provide him with a valuable political education.

01:20:46.260 --> 01:20:47.760
- He was interested in the people.

01:20:47.760 --> 01:20:49.790
He got out and visited with them.

01:20:49.790 --> 01:20:51.010
Even after he was president,

01:20:51.010 --> 01:20:52.950
he would slip away from his bodyguards

01:20:52.950 --> 01:20:57.170
and get out and ride the back ways and back roads

01:20:57.170 --> 01:20:59.600
and meet people, stop and talk with them.

01:21:01.210 --> 01:21:03.460
But he'd never met people like that before.

01:21:03.460 --> 01:21:09.230
He usually talked to you. He started a conversation.

01:21:09.230 --> 01:21:11.840
Whatever he wanted to know, he'd ask.

01:21:11.840 --> 01:21:15.110
He didn't hesitate about asking if he saw something he wanted to know.

01:21:15.110 --> 01:21:27.440
Everywhere he went, he heard stories about the lack of electricity in the countryside

01:21:27.440 --> 01:21:30.000
and the exorbitant rates paid for it in town.

01:21:31.400 --> 01:21:39.100
about bad schools and low farm prices stories that left their mark on him

01:21:39.100 --> 01:21:51.850
i didn't know how come i loved him like i did

01:21:51.850 --> 01:22:00.000
it wouldn't he hadn't done anything for me personally we would walk to warm sprain

01:22:00.670 --> 01:22:08.010
just to see him just board the train. He'd come on down and say, "Hello, Woman's

01:22:08.010 --> 01:22:11.970
Spring. Hello, Woman's Spring." That's the way. We wanted to see him greet the little

01:22:11.970 --> 01:22:18.990
town. And we'd walk four miles to see that. Well, he wasn't the president then. He wasn't

01:22:18.990 --> 01:22:26.230
even the governor of New York, I don't guess. But he was just Mr. Roosevelt in those days.

01:22:26.230 --> 01:22:30.000
After he became president, they were very, you know, polite. But the

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:35.270
They used to call him in the early days Rosie, which I think was a wonderful name.

01:22:35.270 --> 01:22:44.720
Everybody loved, go beyond like they loved.

01:22:44.720 --> 01:22:47.020
I don't think he changed completely.

01:22:47.020 --> 01:22:56.200
There were certain things that were always there, but he certainly learned to understand

01:22:56.200 --> 01:22:59.800
what suffering meant in a way that he'd never known before.

01:23:03.260 --> 01:23:07.910
because he could understand how people could suffer

01:23:07.910 --> 01:23:13.570
in ways that he had not experienced.

01:23:13.570 --> 01:23:27.390
And I think that grew out of his polio experience.

01:23:27.390 --> 01:23:30.000
He certainly gained enormously

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:42.300
in patience. That gave him some of the patience that was needed to meet the problems both

01:23:42.300 --> 01:24:00.000
of the New Deal and the war. People say to him, but if we do this, we don't know.

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:02.000
if we will be successful.

01:24:02.000 --> 01:24:05.200
And I've seen my husband time after time say,

01:24:05.200 --> 01:24:09.410
there are very few things we can know beforehand.

01:24:09.410 --> 01:24:13.190
We will try, and if we find we are wrong,

01:24:13.190 --> 01:24:22.170
we will have to change.

01:24:22.170 --> 01:24:24.530
- Throughout his long struggle with polio,

01:24:24.530 --> 01:24:28.690
Franklin remained determined to return to politics.

01:24:28.690 --> 01:24:30.000
But he knew he would have

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:33.130
to convince voters that he was not an invalid.

01:24:33.130 --> 01:24:36.370
And year after year of arduous exercise

01:24:36.370 --> 01:24:39.220
had not improved his wasted leg muscles.

01:24:39.220 --> 01:24:41.110
- He wanted to be president.

01:24:41.110 --> 01:24:43.620
And it was just unthinkable in those days

01:24:43.620 --> 01:24:45.480
that a person in a wheelchair

01:24:45.480 --> 01:24:48.190
could be elected president of the United States.

01:24:48.190 --> 01:24:51.870
And in fact, it's pretty unthinkable right now.

01:24:51.870 --> 01:24:54.030
And so he had to walk.

01:24:54.030 --> 01:24:55.730
And since he wasn't getting better,

01:24:55.730 --> 01:24:59.470
he developed better techniques for appearing to look better.

01:25:01.280 --> 01:25:08.280
In 1926, physiotherapist Alice Convers taught Franklin how to walk more effectively with crutches.

01:25:09.560 --> 01:25:11.820
He was very anxious to walk.

01:25:11.820 --> 01:25:24.330
He would plant the crutches on the floor so hard you would think the boards would break and then drag himself along.

01:25:25.590 --> 01:25:29.410
It had been five years since the onset of it.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:37.590
His upper body was very strong, but his legs were pretty weak.

01:25:37.590 --> 01:25:51.270
So we tried to get him to use his body muscles in such a way that they would help lift up a leg at a time and take a step.

01:25:51.270 --> 01:25:57.170
But crutches weren't good enough. He knew they were political poison.

01:25:57.170 --> 01:25:59.590
They would, he said, inspire pity.

01:26:00.670 --> 01:26:03.210
He learned instead to appear in public with a cane.

01:26:03.210 --> 01:26:08.590
And he developed this technique that looked like walking.

01:26:09.480 --> 01:26:11.740
His sons were strong men.

01:26:12.020 --> 01:26:15.580
They took exercises so their arms would be as strong as a parallel bar.

01:26:16.540 --> 01:26:21.450
And he would lean on one son's arm, putting all his weight on him.

01:26:22.070 --> 01:26:26.290
And then he would switch his weight from the son's arm onto a cane,

01:26:26.390 --> 01:26:30.000
which he carried in the other hand, so that he could move.

01:26:30.250 --> 01:26:35.110
"switch his weight from side to side and thus progress."

01:26:35.110 --> 01:26:37.110
And he instructed his sons,

01:26:37.110 --> 01:26:40.760
"You must not let people see that this is difficult

01:26:40.760 --> 01:26:43.900
"or takes effort or hurts."

01:26:43.900 --> 01:26:47.740
They would chat and joke and laugh as they went along.

01:26:47.740 --> 01:26:49.320
It was a slow process.

01:26:49.320 --> 01:26:51.280
But they looked as though they were taking their time

01:26:51.280 --> 01:26:52.640
so they could smile at people

01:26:52.640 --> 01:26:55.560
and say hello to the crowd as they went along.

01:26:55.560 --> 01:26:57.560
And it was showbiz, but it worked.

01:27:04.860 --> 01:27:08.140
Only four seconds of film exist which clearly show

01:27:08.140 --> 01:27:17.610
the walk Franklin so tirelessly practiced.

01:27:17.610 --> 01:27:20.530
- The goal really was simply to take enough steps

01:27:20.530 --> 01:27:24.680
to get from a car into a building

01:27:24.680 --> 01:27:28.840
or from his seat on the stage to the podium and back again.

01:27:30.410 --> 01:27:34.490
If he could do that without seeming hopelessly crippled, he'd succeeded.

01:27:35.580 --> 01:27:37.550
Roosevelt had no hip muscles.

01:27:38.090 --> 01:27:43.770
And if a breeze, someone should jostle him, something like that,

01:27:44.000 --> 01:27:45.770
he could just pivot and fall down.

01:27:46.610 --> 01:27:47.970
He was not stable at all.

01:27:48.030 --> 01:27:51.850
It was not a safe way of locomotion, of moving around.

01:27:52.400 --> 01:27:55.290
It was not a practical way, but it was a political way.

01:27:55.290 --> 01:28:00.000
By the summer of 1928, Franklin was ready at last

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:02.630
to make his way back into the political world.

01:28:02.630 --> 01:28:08.000
The Democratic Convention was in Houston,

01:28:08.000 --> 01:28:09.710
and Eleanor had written him,

01:28:09.710 --> 01:28:13.410
"I'm telling everyone you're going to Houston without crutches."

01:28:13.410 --> 01:28:17.000
As he boarded the train for Texas,

01:28:17.000 --> 01:28:21.310
he knew he was about to risk everything.

01:28:36.330 --> 01:28:42.580
through the 1920s, Franklin had kept up his contacts with Democratic Party leaders. Now

01:28:42.580 --> 01:28:49.160
he'd been asked to nominate the governor of New York, Al Smith, for president. Smith

01:28:49.160 --> 01:28:55.460
was a tough, worldly Catholic from New York City, and one of his advisors argued, you're

01:28:55.460 --> 01:29:00.000
a Bowery Mick and he's a Protestant patrician. He'll take some of the curse off you.

01:29:02.970 --> 01:29:05.470
With 15,000 delegates watching,

01:29:05.470 --> 01:29:08.840
Franklin set out to walk to the podium with a cane,

01:29:08.840 --> 01:29:12.660
without the aid of crutches.

01:29:12.660 --> 01:29:15.540
An accidental fall would leave him sprawled helplessly

01:29:15.540 --> 01:29:20.730
on the convention floor, his political hopes destroyed.

01:29:20.730 --> 01:29:23.020
With one hand, he gripped the cane.

01:29:23.020 --> 01:29:25.220
With the other, he balanced precariously

01:29:25.220 --> 01:29:28.210
on his son Elliot's powerful arm.

01:29:28.210 --> 01:29:29.960
He appeared to be walking.

01:29:31.500 --> 01:29:33.380
One reporter described the scene.

01:29:33.380 --> 01:29:40.590
Here on the stage is Franklin Roosevelt, a figure tall and proud even in suffering,

01:29:41.740 --> 01:29:44.280
pale with years of struggle against paralysis,

01:29:45.310 --> 01:29:48.850
a man softened and cleansed and illumined with pain.

01:29:49.620 --> 01:29:52.340
For the moment, we are lifted up.

01:29:54.040 --> 01:29:58.710
We offer one who has the will to win.

01:30:00.440 --> 01:30:05.830
who not only deserves success, but commands it.

01:30:07.950 --> 01:30:11.270
Victory is his habit.

01:30:11.270 --> 01:30:17.370
The happy warrior, Alfred E. Smith.

01:30:31.150 --> 01:30:35.740
The nomination was Al Smith's, but the victory belonged to Franklin Roosevelt.

01:30:36.600 --> 01:30:41.830
When Smith urged him to run for governor of New York, Roosevelt said he was ready.

01:30:48.590 --> 01:30:52.370
Someone asked Smith why he had put Roosevelt back in the political limelight.

01:30:53.370 --> 01:30:56.470
Aren't you raising up a rival who will one day cause you trouble?

01:30:58.140 --> 01:30:59.220
No, Smith replied.

01:31:00.030 --> 01:31:01.310
He'll be dead within a year.

01:31:05.800 --> 01:31:06.660
Six months later,

01:31:06.940 --> 01:31:08.880
Smith had lost his run for the presidency

01:31:08.880 --> 01:31:11.240
and Roosevelt was governor of New York.

01:31:14.040 --> 01:31:16.840
Roosevelt won office by the slimmest of margins,

01:31:17.120 --> 01:31:19.520
campaigning more vigorously than anyone expected.

01:31:21.640 --> 01:31:22.620
Now, as governor,

01:31:22.760 --> 01:31:24.740
he would continue to surprise everyone.

01:31:26.360 --> 01:31:27.880
He took command at once.

01:31:28.720 --> 01:31:30.000
In his first six months,

01:31:30.000 --> 01:31:32.300
He advocated tax relief for farmers

01:31:32.300 --> 01:31:35.460
and cheap electric power for consumers.

01:31:35.460 --> 01:31:38.360
But when disaster suddenly struck the economy,

01:31:38.360 --> 01:31:49.760
no one was sure what Roosevelt would do.

01:31:49.760 --> 01:31:56.330
On October 24th, 1929, the stock market crashed.

01:31:56.330 --> 01:31:58.660
It was the beginning of the worst calamity

01:31:58.660 --> 01:32:00.000
the United States economy

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:06.200
had ever known. Banks closed. Millions were put out of work.

01:32:06.200 --> 01:32:10.720
Homeless people were soon camping just a few blocks from the townhouse Sarah Roosevelt

01:32:10.720 --> 01:32:17.940
had built for Franklin and his bride years before. Eleanor gave instructions to the cook

01:32:17.940 --> 01:32:27.010
to provide anyone who came to the door with hot coffee and sandwiches.

01:32:27.010 --> 01:32:30.000
There's only one word that adequately describes it, and that's

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:39.360
despair a sense of helplessness a sense of hopelessness about a third imagine a

01:32:39.360 --> 01:32:45.740
third of labor totally unemployed 14 million people there was a sense of

01:32:45.740 --> 01:32:56.580
fright sense of horror it was a feeling that what was happening was it possible

01:32:56.580 --> 01:32:59.570
that something like this could occur in the country

01:33:00.540 --> 01:33:04.880
since the start of the depression the republican president herbert hoover

01:33:04.880 --> 01:33:10.180
had settled into a dismal pessimism after one gloomy white house meeting

01:33:10.180 --> 01:33:15.170
his secretary of state said it was like sitting in a bath of ink to sit in his

01:33:15.170 --> 01:33:18.120
room hoover believed there was nothing he

01:33:18.120 --> 01:33:23.410
could do to turn the economy around the crisis would have to resolve itself

01:33:23.410 --> 01:33:28.960
without the aid of government at first roosevelt agreed with hoover

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:34.320
"Industrial and trade conditions are sound," he wired a newspaper the morning after the

01:33:34.320 --> 01:33:37.000
crash.

01:33:37.000 --> 01:33:41.240
But as the crisis deepened, Roosevelt began to change.

01:33:41.240 --> 01:33:46.430
All his life he had believed that relief should come from private charities, but face to face

01:33:46.430 --> 01:33:51.650
with the problems of the Depression, he became convinced that only massive government intervention

01:33:51.650 --> 01:33:54.170
could help.

01:33:54.170 --> 01:34:00.000
For the first time, Roosevelt began to experiment with bold new ideas, assistance for

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:06.600
the agent and the country's first program to provide relief for the unemployed.

01:34:06.600 --> 01:34:11.020
The important thing, he told the New York State Assembly, is to recognize that there

01:34:11.020 --> 01:34:17.460
is a duty on the part of government to do something about this.

01:34:17.460 --> 01:34:23.550
In 1932, President Hoover invited the nation's governors to a White House dinner.

01:34:23.550 --> 01:34:28.960
With his presidency in jeopardy, he wanted to size up the man from New York with the progressive

01:34:28.960 --> 01:34:30.000
programs.

01:34:30.030 --> 01:34:33.440
who is rapidly becoming the Democratic front-runner.

01:34:33.440 --> 01:34:37.470
And the night of the dinner, with a cane in his hand,

01:34:37.470 --> 01:34:39.970
he started going to the dining room,

01:34:39.970 --> 01:34:42.060
dragging his legs from his hips,

01:34:42.060 --> 01:34:46.110
and supporting himself on the cane in his body, guards on him.

01:34:46.110 --> 01:34:49.880
And he walked to the angle, the 45-degree angle of the table,

01:34:49.880 --> 01:34:53.470
and I was alerted to a nod

01:34:53.470 --> 01:34:56.140
that was telling me he was going to take a seat.

01:34:56.140 --> 01:34:59.250
Well, when he did, he literally fell in the seat.

01:35:00.000 --> 01:35:04.760
And that scene was witnessed by all the guests at the dinner table.

01:35:04.760 --> 01:35:09.680
And everybody said, "Well, that man, what is he thinking about? How is he going to be president?"

01:35:09.680 --> 01:35:11.080
He's only a half man.

01:35:15.310 --> 01:35:25.390
On July 1st, 1932, after five tension-filled days at the Democratic National Convention,

01:35:25.390 --> 01:35:29.860
the delegates rallied behind the man who had fought his way back from despair.

01:35:30.640 --> 01:35:37.680
Franklin D. Roosevelt, having received more than two-thirds of all the delegates voting,

01:35:38.170 --> 01:35:43.770
I proclaim him the nominee of this convention for President of the United States.

01:35:45.250 --> 01:35:50.590
Now FDR was ready to begin the race he had been preparing for all his life.

01:35:50.590 --> 01:35:56.150
This is more than a political campaign. It is a call to arms.

01:35:56.550 --> 01:36:00.000
Give me your help not to win votes alone.

01:36:00.920 --> 01:36:04.410
but to win in this crusade!

01:36:04.410 --> 01:36:10.600
To restore America to its own people!

01:36:10.600 --> 01:36:19.300
He refused to let his crippled legs keep him from running hard and with confidence.

01:36:19.300 --> 01:36:24.940
I used to say, if I go to Washington on the 4th of March next,

01:36:24.940 --> 01:36:30.000
but now, at the very nearly the end of this swing,

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:32.420
I'm not saying if, I'm saying when.

01:36:33.430 --> 01:36:35.130
Great war against the Depression,

01:36:36.100 --> 01:36:38.920
being fought on many fronts in many parts of the world.

01:36:39.740 --> 01:36:43.300
His Republican opponent, the President of the United States,

01:36:43.700 --> 01:36:45.680
appeared overwhelmed by the Depression.

01:36:45.680 --> 01:36:48.900
...the last 18 months to carry our financial structures

01:36:48.900 --> 01:36:51.100
safely through the worldwide collapse...

01:36:51.100 --> 01:36:52.420
One observer remarked,

01:36:52.460 --> 01:36:55.980
if you put a rose in Hoover's hand, it would wilt.

01:36:56.640 --> 01:37:00.000
He gave the impression to the American public that...

01:37:00.250 --> 01:37:09.470
He was just out of control, and Roosevelt gave the impression that he knew what the country needed, and he was going to give it to them.

01:37:09.470 --> 01:37:17.450
We face that crisis. We face it with singleness of purpose, and above all, with faith.

01:37:18.270 --> 01:37:26.950
Keep that faith constant. Keep that faith high. So shall we win through to a better day.

01:37:35.720 --> 01:37:38.330
in spite of the crisis the country faced,

01:37:38.330 --> 01:37:41.670
it was a campaign of personalities.

01:37:41.670 --> 01:37:43.990
Americans wanted a leader,

01:37:43.990 --> 01:37:46.960
people everywhere warm to the big smile,

01:37:46.960 --> 01:37:49.290
the confident toss of the head,

01:37:49.290 --> 01:37:50.800
clear delight in people.

01:38:07.580 --> 01:38:18.030
When it was over, Roosevelt had won a smashing victory.

01:38:18.030 --> 01:38:21.650
By midnight, the country already knew

01:38:21.650 --> 01:38:24.050
that Franklin Roosevelt was the winner

01:38:24.050 --> 01:38:26.140
and a very large winner.

01:38:26.140 --> 01:38:30.000
One man sent to Herbert Hoover a wire saying,

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:50.440
for Roosevelt and make it unanimous. I was happy for my husband, Eleanor later

01:38:50.440 --> 01:38:54.810
wrote. I knew that it would make up for the blow that fate had dealt him when he

01:38:54.810 --> 01:39:00.000
was stricken with infantile paralysis. But for my

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:09.060
I was deeply troubled this meant the end of any personal life of my own the turmoil in my heart and mind was rather great that

01:39:09.060 --> 01:39:30.000
Night it would be four months before Roosevelt would take office the worst months yet of the depression 5,000 banks closed

01:39:30.030 --> 01:39:43.820
each month 20,000 farmers lost their land the economy had collapsed Americans

01:39:43.820 --> 01:39:47.180
everywhere waited for the president-elect to tell them what he was going

01:39:47.180 --> 01:39:54.540
to do but Roosevelt gave no clues at one point reporters ask him a question and

01:39:54.540 --> 01:40:00.000
he simply holds up his finger and goes shh he

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:02.140
He will not be drawn out.

01:40:02.900 --> 01:40:09.640
One month before the inauguration, Roosevelt went cruising in the Caribbean with his wealthy friends on Vincent Astor's yacht,

01:40:10.200 --> 01:40:13.830
biggest and fastest ocean-going motor yacht ever built.

01:40:17.230 --> 01:40:21.670
During the campaign, he had promised what he called a new deal for the forgotten man.

01:40:21.750 --> 01:40:26.310
But as yet, he had said nothing about what that new deal might be.

01:40:33.160 --> 01:40:38.500
Meanwhile, Eleanor was expected to give up her teaching and writing to become the nation's first lady.

01:40:38.500 --> 01:40:49.560
...as far here, and forget that there is such a thing as a depression for a time,

01:40:50.320 --> 01:40:58.790
and forget all the troubles that weigh us down, and simply sin.

01:41:00.190 --> 01:41:01.450
is a grand

01:41:01.450 --> 01:41:02.790
kadoop.

01:41:04.050 --> 01:41:26.420
On March 2nd, 1933,

01:41:27.010 --> 01:41:27.980
with the Roosevelts

01:41:27.980 --> 01:41:28.620
on board,

01:41:29.380 --> 01:41:30.000
the Baltimore

01:41:30.000 --> 01:41:32.280
an Ohio train headed toward Washington.

01:41:33.740 --> 01:41:38.440
In two days, Franklin Roosevelt would become the 32nd president of the United States.

01:41:44.510 --> 01:41:46.570
Eleanor sat quietly by herself.

01:41:47.840 --> 01:41:50.960
She feared she was about to lose her hard-won independence.

01:41:53.320 --> 01:41:57.650
I never wanted to be a president's wife, she said, and I don't want it now.

01:42:03.200 --> 01:42:07.730
The president-elect's mother, Sarah, was as always confident in her boy.

01:42:07.730 --> 01:42:11.300
"I am not in the least worried about Franklin," she told a friend.

01:42:11.300 --> 01:42:21.360
In the last car, Franklin Roosevelt sat alone.

01:42:21.360 --> 01:42:26.380
"In all the years I knew him," his son James wrote, "there was only one time

01:42:26.380 --> 01:42:30.000
when father worried about his ability. It was the night he

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:31.020
was elected president.

01:42:32.670 --> 01:42:38.970
You know, Jimmy, he said to me, all my life I have been afraid of only one thing, fire.

01:42:40.120 --> 01:42:42.200
Tonight I think I'm afraid of something else.

01:42:43.890 --> 01:42:45.710
Afraid of what, father, I asked.

01:42:46.510 --> 01:42:50.740
I'm afraid that I may not have the strength to do the job.

01:43:02.520 --> 01:43:11.110
On March 4th, 1933, a man who could not walk would begin to lead a crippled country.

01:43:11.110 --> 01:43:30.000
I, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, do solemnly swear that I will faithfully execute the office of President of the United States and will, to the best of my ability, preserve, protect, and defense.

01:43:30.030 --> 01:43:59.940
The Constitution of the United States, so have me God.

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:07.040
or visit us online at shoppbs.org.
